INT. CENTRAL TELEPHONE EXCHANGE, SWITCHBOARD ROOM - 1912 - NIGHT

Rows of brass jacks. Cords dangle like vines. A gas lamp hisses.

CLEMENTINE VOSS, twenty, headset clamped over a bun, plugs a cord into a jack. Pulls another. Hands blur.

                    CLEMENTINE
          Number, please.

She listens. Smiles, small. Connects the call.

                    CLEMENTINE (CONT'D)
          Go ahead, caller.

A light flares on the board. Another. She works faster.

The door opens. MR. ASHWORTH, fifties, supervisor, in a stiff collar and pince-nez, steps in with a folded paper. He doesn't greet her.

                    ASHWORTH
          Miss Voss.

                    CLEMENTINE
          Number, please. Yes, sir?

                    ASHWORTH
          You were seen leaving the building with a gentleman on Tuesday.

She plugs a cord, fingers steady, eyes on the board.

                    CLEMENTINE
          My brother, sir.

                    ASHWORTH
          You have no brother on the register.

                    CLEMENTINE
          Then the register is wrong.

He sets the paper on her desk. She does not look at it. Another light flares. She answers it.

                    CLEMENTINE (CONT'D)
          Number, please.

                    ASHWORTH
          Miss Voss. I am speaking to you.

                    CLEMENTINE
          And I am speaking to Mrs. Delaney, sir. Her husband is in hospital.

He takes a breath. Straightens his pince-nez. Then he reaches past her, takes the cord, and gently pulls it out of the jack.
